Earlier, Panamanian local media reported that Tehran planned to station its warships at the Panama Canal in an effort to increase its military presence in Latin America. Last week, a State Department spokesman said Washington was keeping a close eye on Iran's naval activities in the Western Hemisphere.
Iran's military presence in the Panama Canal would have angered the United States, which built the canal to connect the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans in the early 20th century. The United States completed the canal in 1914 and established a military base to protect it. The 1977 Treaty paved the way for the transfer of control to the Panamanian government on December 31, 1999.
A total of more than 14,000 ships and vessels crossed the 80-kilometer strait in 2022, according to Panama Canal Authority. The Panama Canal accounts for about 5 percent of the world's maritime trade.
