US trade deficit widens to record high in 2022
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The US trade deficit widened to a new high in 2022, US government data showed on Tuesday. According to the AFP news agency.
The trade deficit widened to 103 billion US dollars by 2022 from 921 to 948.1 billion US dollars due to rising imports, the AFP news agency reported, citing data released by the US Department of Commerce. From crude oil to general consumer goods, including medicines and household products.
According to US government data, the size of the trade deficit is the largest since 1960.
Analysts noted that trade was a factor in last year's gross domestic product (GDP) growth, which slowed the economy in early 2022 but boosted it later.
In December, the trade deficit widened to $ 67.4 billion from $ 6.4 billion, the Commerce Department said.
For December, US imports rose $ 4.2 billion from November to $ 317.6 billion due to rising spending on consumer goods such as mobile phones and consumer goods. In other houses as well as vehicles.
Exports fell from $ 2.2 billion to $ 250.2 billion in December due to lower exports of goods such as industrial supplies and equipment.
Americans are shifting more spending to services instead of consumer goods, with consumers facing a series of high inflation, the AFP news agency reported.
With the baht appreciating last month, foreign investors became net buyers worth more than 58 billion baht in both the stock and debt markets.
The positive outlook is supported by an increase in the number of tourists and the resilience of future exports. According to the Bangkok Post.
