Britain: Risi Sunak reshuffles cabinet to boost economic crisis
LONDON (Reuters) - British Prime Minister Rishi Sunak on Tuesday reshuffled his cabinet, splitting ministries and creating new ones to live up to his promise to boost the economy. Reduce energy prices and boost confidence for his party ahead of the 2024 elections. This is according to the publication by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, February 8, 2023.
Sunak has set up a new Ministry of Energy Security, headed by former Commerce Minister Grant Shapps, as well as three other ministries, partly focusing on science and innovation, which is his personal passion. The new Ministry of Energy Security will help the UK gain more energy and start producing cheaper clean energy to avoid rising prices, which cost people more after Russia's invasion of Ukraine.
"The government needs to reflect the priorities of the British people by trying to provide for them," Sunak said on Twitter. "These changes will focus on problem-solving teams that will build a better future for the next generation."
The British leader, a former finance minister and billionaire who used to work for a hedge agency, is trying to tackle a long-stagnant economy as the situation worsens due to energy prices. Increase. Sunak is also trying to show that the UK can benefit from leaving the EU (Brexit) by establishing new trade relations with international partners.
