(Syria): An earthquake in Turkey has killed thousands and injured tens of thousands. The incident happened at low light on Monday, when people were sleeping soundly.
The death toll from the quake has risen to more than 5,000, including more than 3,400,000 in Turkey and more than 1,600 in Syria. That number is expected to continue to rise as rescuers continue to search for people trapped in the rubble. A magnitude 7.8 earthquake shook Turkey at 4 a.m. Monday, shaking Lebanon and Israel.
Rescuers are still searching for people trapped underground, the report said on Tuesday. Some people were rescued and a newborn baby was rescued from the rubble. The boy's mother had died in the incident. Pictures on social media showed a little girl being rescued from the rubble. A man was rescued from a pile of piles.
In Syria, a country plagued by civil war, the death toll from the quake has added to the tragedy of bloody wars between fighters of their own nation. In the areas controlled by the opposition, 740 people were killed, while in government-controlled areas, 711 people were killed.
UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres has made an overnight call for humanitarian assistance to the quake victims. "Let us unite to help those affected by this earthquake, many of whom are in dire need of assistance," said the UN Secretary-General.
The United States reportedly responded immediately by cooperating with Turkey to provide humanitarian assistance and rescue teams. Many countries have continued to provide assistance to people affected by the quake.
Erdogan said the exact number of dead and injured was not yet known. The death toll and injuries will continue to rise as rescuers are busy rescuing people from the rubble of buildings below them. "Our hope is that we will survive this catastrophe with the least loss of life," he said.
UN envoy pledges economic growth in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): UN representatives pledge to boost Cambodia's economic growth and support Cambodia in leaving the status of a least developed country.
The Minister informed the delegation of the United Nations Coordinating Office that in addition to the efforts to recover the economy after the Kovid-19 crisis, the Royal Government of Cambodia has been working to promote further growth and socio-economic development. Can meet the criteria in the second three-year inspection in 2024.
The Royal Government, including the Ministry of Commerce, is ready to work closely with the United Nations Coordinating Office and other development partners to develop and implement detailed strategies and work plans for leaving the Least Developed Countries (LDCs). Have a smooth and successful departure.
The Minister also highlighted other important developments in the trade sector, including the development of e-commerce in Cambodia to increase the resilience of the supply chain and efforts to examine the feasibility and registration of geographical indications. (GI) For some commodities in the fisheries sector, including Siem Reap prahok.
Mr. Pan Sosak also thanked UNIDO for its continued support and technical assistance in studying the feasibility of registering geographical markers for these goods.
Mr. Joseph Scheuer highlighted the continued support for Cambodia in the process of preparing to leave the status of a least developed country, especially the continued promotion of environmental growth, focusing on the participation of micro and small and medium enterprises and the participation of women entrepreneurs. And support for the Royal Government in establishing a digitalization nursery.
Both sides reaffirmed their commitment to work closely together to implement existing projects, initiatives and new programs aimed at achieving the Royal Government of Cambodia's socio-economic development agenda and inclusive development goals. By sustainability.
