The new force is a rotating force with Engineering Force No. 829, which has returned to Cambodia after completing a successful one-year mission and bringing national prestige.
The departure and reception ceremony for the Cambodian Blue Hat Army was presided over by General Sem Sovanny, Vice Chairman of the National Committee and Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, as well as the leadership of the National Center at Phnom Penh International Airport.
General Sem Sovanny stated that the mission in the Republic of Lebanon was the 13th year and that the 829th Engineering Brigade was the 12th to participate in a fruitful mission with proud results. At the same time, the unit maintains cleanliness, good discipline, and strict adherence to UN standards, with safety first and foremost, achieving success without any members being injured or sacrificed for any reason.
As for the forces to be shifted, General Sem Sovanny reminded all forces to continue to unite as one and to consider safety first, especially during the performance of duties, must not underestimate any situation and must learn from experience and Good lessons correct any mistakes that have been made to achieve success for the motherland, family and yourself.
At the same time, General Sem Sovanny expressed his pleasure at the Cambodian forces who completed a one-year mission in the Middle East, which has a sub-zero temperature different from Cambodia and a mountainous area under economic pressure. The border guards between Israel and their families are far from being invaluable to the Cambodian army.
Cambodia ranks second in ASEAN and 12th among all countries that send Blue Hat troops to countries around the world.
From 2006 to 2023, Cambodia has decided to send troops to more than 8,000 peacekeeping missions in nine countries, including Sudan, South Sudan, Chad, Central Africa, Lebanon, Cyprus, Syria, Mali and Yemen.
