For 2023, Indonesia will hold the rotating chairmanship of ASEAN and will host the annual ASEAN Leaders' Summit this year. Myanmar has become one of the main problems for ASEAN since the military coup in February 2021, pushing the country into chaos.
Indonesian Foreign Minister Retno Marsudi said on Saturday that Jakarta had proposed an action plan to ASEAN members for a "five points of consensus" agreed with Myanmar's military government in April. 2021, which calls for an end to the violence and the establishment of dialogue between the military regime and the opposition.
Masudi said the plan was widely supported by all member states, but did not specify when or how they hoped the agreement would be implemented. However, the Indonesian diplomat added that the plan was very important for ASEAN, especially for the chair, to resolve the situation in Myanmar in a unified manner.
Myanmar's military government has been barred from attending the ASEAN summit for failing to implement a five-point peace plan. Myanmar's foreign minister was not present during the two-day meeting in Jakarta, as ASEAN did not invite members of the military regime, requesting that Myanmar send only non-political representatives, but Nay Pyi Taw refused.
