The discovery of the balloon prompted US Secretary of State Anthony Blinken to suspend a rare visit to Beijing this weekend. Ahead of the cancellation of the visit, which was aimed at easing tensions between the two countries, China issued a statement expressing regret and blaming the air for bringing in a balloon it claimed to be a civilian balloon for meteorological research. To U.S. airspace.
On Saturday, the Chinese Foreign Ministry issued another statement. "China has never violated the territory and airspace of any sovereign state," the statement said. "Some politicians and the media in the United States have taken the opportunity to use the incident as an excuse to attack and tarnish China's image."
The Chinese Foreign Ministry said that the incident was an unforeseen accident and that it has always adhered to international law and respected the sovereignty and integrity of countries. Beijing added that maintaining communication at all levels is important, especially to address some unpredictable situations in a trustworthy and non-violent manner.
