Speaking to reporters after the ASEAN Foreign Ministers' Meeting in Jakarta, Masudi affirmed that member states are committed to holding talks on the South China Sea Code of Conduct as soon as possible. According to the Indonesian diplomat, the next round of talks on the issue will be held in March.
Much of the South China Sea is under Chinese claims, with some areas overlapping with ASEAN countries such as Brunei, Malaysia, the Philippines and Vietnam, while Indonesia is not involved in the dispute. ASEAN countries have been trying for years to negotiate a code of conduct with China aimed at resolving disputes and preventing incidents in the South China Sea.
The South China Sea Code of Conduct negotiations have stalled for various reasons, most recently due to the emergence of the 19-Kovid epidemic, which has made it difficult to hold face-to-face talks. This Friday-Saturday meeting in Jakarta is the first major ASEAN meeting on the occasion of Indonesia's rotating chairmanship this year.
