UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon seeks to mobilize resources from overseas sources for humanitarian mine action in Cambodia
At the same time, he would like to participate in mobilizing resources from foreign sources for humanitarian mine action in Cambodia. The United Nations will continue to support this demining work and hope that Cambodia will be able to achieve its goal of a mine-free Cambodia by 2025.
This statement was made on February 2, 2023 at the Mine Authority Office when he joined Senior Minister Ly Thuch, First Vice President of the Mine Authority as a partner and cooperation in mine action in Cambodia.
Senior Minister Ly Thuch thanked the United Nations for supporting the Royal Government of Cambodia's efforts to address landmines and UXO by supporting the inclusion of mine and UXO clearance activities. Enter the ninth goal of the Millennium Development Goals (CMDG) and the 18th goal of the Sustainable Development Goals of Cambodia (CSDG).
He added that after the end of the war, the Cambodian government under the high leadership of Samdech Techo Prime Minister considered mine action as a priority to address and contribute to the development of the country. Currently, Cambodian mine action has reached a turning point and achieved many historical achievements over the past 30 years in liberating the land for the people safely and contributing to the socio-economic development.
He said that Cambodia has received support for the clearance of mines and explosive remnants of war from many different sources, including the Royal Government, development partners and philanthropists, as we can clearly see from 2022, Samdech Techo Decided to establish the Samdech Techo Project for the Mine Action and Fund of Cambodia 2025 to expedite demining work by 2025.
On behalf of the Royal Government, the Senior Minister considered the United Nations as a close friend of Cambodia in the process of seeking peace and development in Cambodia, as well as supporting mine action in Cambodia. On that occasion, the Senior Minister invited the President of the United Nations in Cambodia to inspect and find out about the demining activities of operators in the field directly at any appropriate time.
A Cambodian female worker went to sell vegetables in Thailand and fell seriously ill with a broken kidney on both sides.
(Thailand, Bangkok): A Cambodian woman worker went to work as a vegetable seller in a market in Bangkok, Thailand, and fell seriously ill with kidney damage on both sides, the last stage is seeking help from philanthropists. Possibility to treat the disease.
Born in Bavel district, Battambang province, a 27-year-old woman named Yoeut Thinh migrated to Thailand for a living, but within a few years she suddenly fell ill.
The family of the young woman told Fresh News on February 2, 2023 that a medical examination in Thailand confirmed that the woman suffered a last kidney damage. But the condition can only be cured by having a kidney transplant in India when the family cannot afford it.
In connection with the recent case of Ms. Yoeut Thinh, Mr. Oknha Leng Pros, General Director of Navatra Giant Company, while traveling to Thailand, also met Ms. Yoeut Tinh in person at the market where he sells vegetables. At that time, the tycoon also donated some money to the women to contribute to the treatment.
