Mr. HUN Manet Provides Medical Equipment to General Department of Prisons at Prey Sar Polytechnic
(Phnom Penh): Lt. Gen. Dr. Hun Manet has assigned a team of Samdech Techo volunteer doctors to bring medical equipment to the General Department of Prisons of the Ministry of Interior to expand health services in prisons.
The ceremony of providing the above medical equipment was held on February 2, 2023 at Prey Sar Polytechnic Hall in the presence of the First Superintendent of Class 1, Chhem Savuth, Director General of the General Department of Prisons, Ministry of Interior, with the participation of the Deputy Director General of the Department. Prison officials, including a group of Samdech Techo youth volunteer doctors.
Medical equipment provided to the General Department of Prisons includes: 33 sets of hospital beds, 99 sets of wheelchairs, 63 sets of crutches, 63 sets of PP50 immunization equipment and 20,000 Kovid 19 rapid test kits.
Mr. Chhem Savuth said, "On behalf of the leadership of the General Department of Prisons and on my own behalf, I am extremely proud that the General Department of Prisons has received medical equipment from HE Dr. HUN Manet. "His Excellency always pays attention to the health of the general population as well as the health of prisoners throughout the country."
He said that the prison sector in Cambodia has a history of going through difficult challenges from the beginning, but with the Rectangular Strategy Phase 4 of the Royal Government and the prison policy reform framework of the Ministry of Interior, we strive to reduce all difficulties. Gradually promote the progress of Cambodia's prisons.
The evolution of society from year to year, with the recent enforcement of legal measures, has created a crisis of overcrowding beyond the prison capacity of prisons, putting general prisons under pressure from work, security, safety and efficiency. Implement the rehabilitation of detainees, especially the well-being of officials and detainees. Related to health issues and infectious diseases in prisons are challenges that require us to address together.
He added that in response to the above challenges, we have set out to implement, including: building and expanding infrastructure to alleviate overcrowding, training health officials, and improving health services in prisons. Requesting inter-ministerial principles to expand the health post level to the health center in the first correctional education center and to expand from the health office level to the prison health department under the General Department of Prisons.
Finally, on behalf of the General Department of Prisons, I would like to express my deepest gratitude to Dr. HUN Manet and his wife for their continued support for the prison health sector and for continuing to support the prison sector in Cambodia. Has improved even more.
