IMF raises economic forecasts for emerging markets
NEW YORK (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) on Monday raised its forecast for the country's emerging market output for the year on the back of China's reopening, resilience to the Indian economy and sluggish growth. Russian expectations. According to Reuters.
Inflation, which has been a stumbling block in recent economic growth, has been seen as high, although it will continue to decline this year and next. Emerging and developing markets are expected to see inflation rise to 9.9 percent in 2022, then to 8.1 percent in 2023 and 5.5 percent in 2024. But still higher than the average rate of 4.9 percent between 2017 and 2019.
Reuters reports that 15 percent of low-income countries are already estimated to be in debt crisis, and another 45 percent are at high risk of going into debt crisis by one in four countries. As a growing market, there are also high risks.
In 2023, India's economy is expected to grow by more than 6 percent this year and next, while China's economic growth forecast of 0.8 percentage points puts the country on track. Towards achieving economic growth above 5 percent this year.
Russia, meanwhile, is projected to grow 2.6 percentage points in its 2023 economic growth forecast, which means it will grow 0.3 percent this year. According to Reuters.
