General Knowledge: Who is the Father of Cambodian Democracy?
(Phnom Penh): As you know, there are two major regimes in the world: democracy and socialism. Democracy was first established in the 6th century BC, after the overthrow of monarchies in the Greek world. Socialism, or communism, began to practice about 25 centuries after democracy.
Today, Cambodia is a monarchy led by a liberal, multi-party democracy. Do you know who is the father of Cambodian democracy?
Sisowath Yutthivong is a Cambodian prince, politician and mathematician who is considered the "Father of Cambodian Democracy" and a member of the Democratic Party. He was the fourth Prime Minister of Cambodia from December 1946 to July 1947. He was the first person to be elected prime minister.
Sisowath Yutthivong was born in 1913 in Odong, Cambodia to a father named Chamroeun Vong, a grandson of King Phim, a mother named Yupiphon, a grandson of King Sisowath.
The Democrat Party, led by Prince Yutthivong, won the September 1946 election. This election is marked as the first democratic election in the history of Cambodia. Prince Yutthivong was sworn in as Prime Minister on December 15, 1946.
Prince Yutthivong was hailed by Cambodian political leaders at the time as the "Father of Democracy in Cambodia." It is often said, especially in secondary education, that Prince Sisowath Yutthivong was the first to bring democracy to Cambodia.
Prince Sisowath Yutthivong did not live long. He died on July 17, 1947 at the age of 34 (35) in Preah Ketomealea Hospital due to illness.
After the death of Prince Sisowath Yutthivong and the death of Iev Kes, the Democrat Party, which had been prominent in Cambodian politics in the 1940s, gradually began to disintegrate, almost completely disappearing in the decades. 1960 and was succeeded by Sisowath Vathanavong.
Fragments of the stupa burying the remains of Prince Sisowath Yutthivong in the compound of Wat Salakou, Ponhea Leu District, Kandal Province. Prince Sisowath Yutthivong was the first founder of the Constitution of Cambodia, who died on August 11, 1947.
Prince Yutthivong was married to a French woman named Dominique Laverne and had two daughters, Sisowath Kuntheara, born in 1945 and Sisowath Linda, born in 1946. Chief of Staff of Koh Kong Provincial Gendarmerie Command chaired a meeting to review the implementation of weekly duties of all specialized departments.
