(Phnom Penh): Experts from the Institute of Science and Technology of the Royal Academy of Cambodia organized a workshop to present the detailed results of the research project on "Application of Bio-Ocha with Bio-Compost by Local Technology and Smart Agriculture on Vegetable Crops in Cambodia".
The workshop was held on January 27, 2023, with presentations by experts, as well as questions about the importance of the application of bioha with bio compost on vegetable crops.
This project was carried out by Dr. Mam Sarith, Research Officer of the Institute of Science and Technology of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, as the project director, "Implementing Bio-Cha with Bio-Compost by Local Technology and Smart Agriculture on Vegetable Crops in Cambodia." The project runs from March to November 2022. Funded by the Royal Government through the Royal Academy of Cambodia.
Dr. Mam Sarith holds a PhD from the National University of Seoul, Republic of Korea (South Korea). Please be informed that Seoul National University is ranked No. 1 in Korea and No. 29 in the world (WUR 2022).
Dr. stated that the world's main ideas for the benefits of biochar are: first: to reduce climate change, second: energy products for absorbing nutrients or heavy metals in the soil without compromising health, third : Improving soil quality and fourth: waste management.
Dr. Mam Sarith said that the benefits of repairing damaged soil caused by the use of chemical fertilizers in excess of poor quality fertilizers. The role of bioha when mixed into the soil is to retain moisture in the soil, not to decompose (for hundreds of years) as a habitat for microorganisms, increase soil pests such as earthworms, make the soil more fertile for agricultural crops and Make oxygen in the soil.
In addition, Dr. expects that the benefit for farmers and consumers is to get a healthy agricultural product (vegetable or fruit), farmers can produce bio by simple technology, and the price is the most reasonable self-reduction Importing chemical fertilizers from abroad is a good opportunity to clean up the city by recycling waste and getting organic vegetables for the health of our people.
Dr. Mam Sarith said that on behalf of the National Research Institute and on behalf of the experts, he was happy to receive all kinds of consultations on supporting the technology of production and implementation of bio-o to improve the quality of the soil to increase the yield of agricultural crops in Cambodia.
