(Seoul, Korea): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, requested the South Korean Customs Authority to sign a memorandum of understanding with the Cambodian Customs to promote better trade between Cambodia and South Korea.
During the meeting, Mr. Pan Sosak thanked the South Korean Customs Authority for inviting him to participate in the working food program on the 41st International Customs Day organized by the Korean Customs Authority.
The Minister praised the close cooperation between the Cambodian and Korean customs authorities, which facilitated export-import activities between the two countries. In addition, the Minister also highlighted the importance of the Cambodia-Korea Free Trade Agreement, which entered into force in December 2022, which will contribute significantly to the promotion of trade, investment and economic cooperation. Between the two countries as well.
Meanwhile, Minister Pan Sosak urged the Korean Customs Authority to consider the possibility of signing a memorandum of understanding with the General Department of Customs and the Cambodian Customs in the near future to enhance cooperation and provide further coordination after Delayed due to Kovid-19 crisis.
In addition, the Minister of Commerce also requested the Korean Customs Authority to continue to support Cambodia through training and capacity building programs for Cambodian officials.
The head of the Korean Customs Authority also welcomed and supported the increase in close cooperation between the two countries' customs authorities. He said he would look into the possibility of signing a memorandum of understanding on customs between the two countries in the near future to boost trade and investment between the two countries.
In addition, the President of the Korean Customs Authority also supported the Minister's request and will continue to share knowledge, experience and capacity building to relevant Cambodian officials.
