Thailand's law on insulting the monarch is extremely strict, with each offense punishable by up to 15 years in prison. A 29-year-old man named Mongkul Tirakot, who sells clothes online, has been convicted of two counts of insulting the King in northern Chiang Rai. Separate.
According to his own lawyer, Mongkul was initially sentenced to a total of 42 years in prison, but the court reduced his sentence to 28 years after his testimony. The lawyer added that Mongkul planned to appeal the verdict as the court granted him bail of 300,000 baht, or nearly $ 10,000.
Mongkul also faces a third separate charge of insulting the King over last year's online messaging and will return to court in March. According to human rights groups, his 28-year sentence is the second-longest sentence for insulting the monarch in Thailand.
In 2021, a Thai court sentenced a woman to a record 43 years in prison for insulting the King, while she initially faces up to 87 years in prison. Remains in prison.
