(Seoul, Korea): While leading a Cambodian delegation to the Cambodia-Korea Business and Investment Forum in Seoul, South Korea, Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak met with relevant South Korean authorities to promote Better trade between Cambodia and South Korea.
On the afternoon of January 26, 2023, Mr. Pan Sosak led a delegation from Cambodia and relevant private sectors to meet with Mr. Byung-Kwan Kim, President of the Korean Importers Association and his colleagues in order to promote trade and investment in Cambodia. . Mr. Sok Chenda Sophea, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Secretary General of the Council for the Development of Cambodia was also present at the meeting.
The Minister of Commerce encouraged the members of the Korea Importers Association to take advantage of the free trade agreement that Cambodia and Korea are members of, including the ASEAN-Korea Free Trade Agreement, the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement and The Cambodia-Korea Free Trade Agreement, which entered into force on December 1, 2022, as well as the incentives under the new investment law.
The Minister of Commerce stressed that while the trade agreements provide favorable markets and favorable conditions for producers, exporters and importers of both Cambodia and Korea to promote trade and mutual investment. It will also play an important role in promoting economic, trade and investment cooperation between the two countries.
The President of the Korea Importers Association warmly welcomed the presence of the Minister and the delegation, who took the time to visit and discuss work at the Korea Importers Association's headquarters in Seoul.
He expressed optimism that these free trade agreements would further boost trade, economic and investment between the two countries. The President will urge members of the Korean Importers Association to examine the possibility of importing Cambodian goods to the Korean market and seek more partners in Cambodia, especially to take advantage of the Cambodia-Korea Bilateral Free Trade Agreement.
During the meeting, the relevant private sectors of the two countries introduced their business objects and shared information on trade, business operations and potential sectors / goods of interest for trade cooperation between Cambodia and Korea. As well as future investment.
Cambodia aims to increase automotive exports to about $ 500 million by 2027 (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has launched a roadmap for the development of the automotive and electronics sectors through this roadmap. The Royal Government aims to increase the export volume in the automotive sector to about 500 million US dollars by 2027 and create 10,000 new jobs in this sector.
Currently, Japan is a major source of investment in the automotive sector in Cambodia, while the main export markets include Thailand and Japan.
At the same time, Cambodia aims to attract more of the world's leading auto parts manufacturers and possibly create a more complete ecosystem to support those manufacturers, including local suppliers. From low to medium.
Cambodia can make the most of its current competitive advantages, such as skilled labor and competitive wages, strategic locations near Thailand and Vietnam, centers for the rapid development of automobiles and electronics, and trade agreements. Convenient and favorable with vehicle export market.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Permanent Secretary of State for the Ministry of Economy and Finance, said that the Royal Government has set up a state-owned professional development fund and in collaboration with companies to train skilled workers for the electronics and automotive industries in Cambodia. Through this fund, the Royal Government will provide some funding for this training.
"The budget for technical skills is increasing year by year, but we do not just want to increase the money, we want the efficiency and quality of the skills, and it responds to both the market and the skills."
