TOKYO: Japan has lowered its outlook for the economy as a whole for the first time in 11 months as the Kovid-19 outbreak in China and declining demand for global technology and electronics have hurt exports. Especially to Asia. According to Reuters.
The Japanese government expects the world's third-largest economy to continue to grow, but needs to pay close attention to the impact of the epidemic in China after the country cut off, Reuters reported. Reduce strict measures to prevent this epidemic.
The downgrade comes after the Bank of Japan last week cut its growth forecast for the next two fiscal years amid worries that a slowdown in global demand could hurt the economy. Affects Japan's export-dependent economy.
"The economy is recovering moderately, but some declines have been seen recently," according to the latest cabinet report.
Japanese authorities reduced their export appraisal for the first time since November 2011 and also cut their import appraisal for the first time in three months. Reuters reports that both exports and imports are forecast to be declining recently compared to last month's outlook, which is almost stagnant.
"The resurgence of Kovid-19 cases in China could affect Japan's exports and production, and such a possibility is clearer than last month," said an official from the Japanese Cabinet Office.
The Japanese government remains cautious about the downside risks of a global economic slowdown amid tightening currency, inflation and volatile financial markets. According to Reuters.
