Minister of Commerce asks Britain to invest in agricultural processing
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, requested the United Kingdom to invest in the processing of agricultural products, food and agro-industry in Cambodia.
The request was made on the occasion of Minister Pan Sosak's courtesy call and discussion with newly appointed British Ambassador to Cambodia Dominic WILLIAMS. The meeting was held to exchange views and discuss the progress of bilateral economic and trade cooperation.
In this meeting, Minister Pan Sosak warmly welcomed the Ambassador and his colleagues and thanked the people and the Royal Government of the United Kingdom for always providing financial support and technical assistance to Cambodia in the field of potential. These include: trade, health, education, financial management, infrastructure development, and Kovid-19 vaccine donation.
The Minister expressed his satisfaction with the implementation of the new UK preferential treatment offered to less developed countries, including Cambodia. This provision has contributed to supporting Cambodian businesses and investors, promoting exports, increasing employment opportunities and developing the country in an environmentally resilient and sustainable environment, especially in contributing to the economic recovery from the Kovid-19 crisis.
During the meeting, Mr. Pan Sosak and the British Ambassador noted the excellent cooperation between Cambodia and the United Kingdom, especially the increase in bilateral trade volume, as well as the willingness to sign the Memorandum of Understanding. Agree on the establishment of a joint trade and investment forum between the two countries in the near future to promote investment and the growth of bilateral trade.
Mr. Pan Sosak and the Ambassador reaffirmed their commitment to further strengthen the relationship between the two countries, especially the economic and trade cooperation to be stronger and deeper.
The United Kingdom is an important market for Cambodia's agricultural and manufacturing products, with bilateral trade reaching $ 1 billion by 2022, an increase of 24% compared to 2021. Which reached 786 million US dollars.
Potential goods that Cambodia exports to the UK include: clothing, footwear, bicycles and agricultural products, while potential imports from the UK to Cambodia include machinery, pharmaceuticals and beverages. Dr. HUN Manet and Mrs. sent a representative to visit Mr. Bun Sam, a senior CPP official at his home
