BANGKOK: Analysts say a surge in tourism from China's reopening of the country will boost Thailand's economy, but not enough to restore growth to pre-epidemic levels. No, not at all. According to Bloomberg News.
This is because economists expect the number of Chinese tourist arrivals to be around half to 10 million, which the Thai government estimates will visit Thailand this year. Nine economists surveyed say Thailand's tourism sector is beginning to fully recover to its pre-Kovid crisis from 2024.
About 28% of the 40 million tourists who visited Thailand before the outbreak came from China. Tourism accounts for at least 12% of the Thai economy and creates one-fifth of employment, while private consumption accounts for 50% of GDP. Together with exports, which account for more than half of economic output, these two sectors form the lifeblood of the Thai economy.
