Excavations on the west side of Angkor Wat find artifacts and historical facts
(Siem Reap): The Apsara Authority and the University of Hawaii have collaborated to study the evolution of Theravada Buddhism, focusing on the evolution of local communities from pre-Angkorian to the Middle Ages. Some when the pagoda replaced the Angkorian temples, becoming the core of the community.
Prior to the excavation at this site, experts used LiDAR technology to scan the air and also found a house on the northwest side of Angkor Wat in the 12th century was demolished around the Middle Ages.
Mr. Tina explained that the data obtained from this excavation allows us to understand the human community living in Angkor, which may respond to a number of major historical factors, such as the transition to Buddhism, the decline of central power. Angkor The shifting of the capital to the south (around Phnom Penh) and the brief return of central power in the 16th century of the Longvek dynasty.
He added that in 2019-2020, the research team of both institutions also conducted excavations in Daun Pok, Kork Bak and Kork Kong in the area of Wat Athva in Kork Roka and Kork Prey Ampil, born in the area of Wat Chet and in Kork Prey Ampil Lech in the area. Wat Prey Pdao. The purpose of this excavation is to collect data from communities living in rural areas of Angkor.
Please be informed that since 2013, the research team has excavated on the eastern hill of Angkor Wat, they found grapefruit peel, rice and cotton seeds left over from ancient times.
