(Svay Rieng): On the morning of January 20, 2023, Dr. Ouk Rabun, Minister of Rural Development, led a delegation to inspect and evaluate a 11-kilometer and 90-meter (DBST) double-paved road. Which is scheduled to be completed in early August 2023.
This road is connected from National Road No. 18 at O Don Am village, Preah Ponlea commune to Samyong village, Samyong commune, Kampong Rou district, Svay Rieng province.
The inspection was attended by Mr. Sok Ros, Deputy Governor of Svay Rieng Province, Deputy Governor, Mr. Kim Thea, Senator Mr. Sam Nito, Director of Svay Rieng Provincial Department of Rural Development and Kampong Ror District Governor, residents and local authorities. Also.
He added that the third phase of the rural road improvement project is a project of the Royal Government managed by the Ministry of Rural Development to improve the red gravel road in the countryside into a two-story asphalt road type DBST and concrete road to make it easier to build. People travel and transportation.
The project aims to improve the travel of people living in the 10 target provinces, easy access to markets, job search, easy access to social services and focus on social safety, resilience and climate. And make it efficient to travel in all seasons in areas with a high agricultural scope.
Dr. Ouk Rabun praised and thanked the representatives of the company that built this road well and called on local authorities at all levels, people with housing on the streets to cooperate. Good with the construction contractor and instructed the technical officer of the company stationed at the site to comply with the design and technical standards to ensure both quality and aesthetics.
It should be noted that the 10 target provinces of the project, including Kampong Cham, Tbong Khmum, Kandal, Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Speu, Takeo, Kampot, Prey Veng, Svay Rieng and Kratie provinces, will receive DBST and 724 km concrete roads with a total length of 724 km from the Royal Government of Cambodia through the Ministry Rural development within the framework of the Rural Road Improvement Project, Phase 3.
The project will be implemented from 2020 to 2025 with a total budget of 120 million US dollars, including concessional financing from the Asian Development Bank, the Export-Import Bank of the Republic of Korea and the contribution of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
