Cambodian diplomat to pay official visit to Japan this weekend
(Phnom Penh): Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of the Kingdom of Cambodia, will lead a delegation on an official working visit to Japan from January 21 to 24, 2023. This is according to the announcement of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, which was received by Fresh News.
According to the Foreign Ministry, Prak Sokhon will meet with Hayashi Yoshimasa, where bilateral and multilateral cooperation as well as regional and international issues of common concern will be discussed.
During his stay in Japan, the Deputy Prime Minister will have separate meetings with the Japan-Cambodia Friendship Parliamentary Alliance and the Japan-Cambodia Association. He will also preside over a party commemorating the 70th anniversary of the establishment of Cambodia-Japan diplomatic relations at the Cambodian Embassy in Tokyo.
The Ministry of Foreign Affairs emphasized that Mr. Prak Sokhon's upcoming visit to Japan will continue the strong commitment of the two governments to strengthen the existing good relations and comprehensive strategic partnership. Which was recently installed for the mutual benefit of the two countries.
The number of international tourists worldwide will double in 2022
A total of 917 million world tourists visited the country last year, up from 455 million in 2021, AFP reported, citing a report released by the World Tourism Organization based in Madrid, Spain. "The results were much higher than expected."
While the number of international tourists last year remained at 63 percent of the level announced in 2019 ahead of the Kovid-19 outbreak, the United Nations estimates it could rise to 80 percent to 95 percent. In 2023.
International tourism is expected to recover in 2023, supported by rising demand, especially from Asia and the Pacific, an open destination and market, the report said. "In the short term, the resumption of travel by China is likely to benefit Asian destinations," the report added.
