International Analysis: Why is the South Korean President talking about "nuclear weapons"?
If so, why is Yun talking about nuclear weapons now? What exactly do you want? In a joint meeting with leaders of the South Korean Ministry of Defense and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs on Wednesday, Yun stressed that the development of nuclear weapons has not yet been incorporated or become a policy. And that will continue to address the North Korean nuclear threat by strengthening alliances with the United States.
South Korea is a signatory to the Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT), which prohibits Seoul from developing nuclear weapons. So if South Korea wants to acquire nuclear weapons, it must first withdraw from the treaty. But according to analysts, withdrawal from the NPT could pose a high risk for South Korea, which could face international sanctions.
