Most of the trade followed global GDP growth during the decade before the outbreak, the report said. Thus, the report predicts the worst stagnation in world trade since the WTO was formed more than 25 years ago.
Under the turmoil highlighted by BCG, the next nine years of trade turmoil will create the following winners and losers.
The EU will increase its trade with the United States by $ 338 billion, largely driven by US energy exports to Europe, and will also expand joint trade with ASEAN countries, Africa, the Middle East and India.
Trade between the United States and China will shrink by $ 63 billion, and for trade between the EU and China, trade growth will also slow to just $ 72 billion, which BCG calls an "increase." "A little higher compared to last year."
Russia's trade with China and India will increase by $ 110 billion, "including $ 90 billion with China alone," the consultant said.
Southeast Asia will be the biggest winner, with an estimated $ 1 trillion in new trade with China, Japan, the United States and the European Union.
China's reopening could boost oil demand to new heights
LONDON (Reuters) - The lifting of restrictions on Kovid-19 in China is set to boost global oil demand this year to a new high as the International Atomic Energy Agency (IEA) said on Wednesday. Russian oil prices could cut supplies According to Reuters.
"Two factors overshadow the 2023 oil market outlook, Russia and China," Reuters reported, citing the Paris-based IEA monthly report.
"Russia's supplies fall below the full impact of sanctions (while) China will boost global demand growth by almost half, despite the shape and pace of reopening," the IEA added. Still uncertain. ”
Weak industrial activity and inclement weather helped reduce oil demand by nearly a million barrels a day in the Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) member countries in the last quarter of 2022.
Despite the recession in Europe and the United States, China's reopening, as expected, could boost the economic recovery of its Asian neighbors, and India will bring Leading as the world's fastest growing oil demand.
Russia's oil output fell just 200,000 barrels a day (bpd) in December after the European Union banned the import of crude oil by sea and imposed tariffs on Russia, the IEA said. According to Reuters.
