Cambodia urges US government to continue supporting demining by 2025 and increase funding for cluster munitions clearance
The Senior Minister welcomed the meeting and expressed his deep gratitude to the Government and people of the United States of America for their support of mine and UXO clearance in Cambodia for more than 30 years in providing Safe and save the lives of local people.
The Senior Minister highlighted the progress and efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia in dealing with landmines and explosive remnants of war in Cambodia, setting out a clear strategic plan to complete demining by 2025. He stressed that Cambodia is optimistic and sincerely hopes that Cambodia will be able to achieve its goal of mine-free 2025 as planned.
He added that in addition to demining, cluster munitions are Cambodia's second priority goal as stated in the National Strategic Plan for Mine Action 2018-2025, with Cambodia remaining 735 square kilometers of suspected cluster munitions. .
On behalf of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the Senior Minister requested that the United States Government continue to support demining by 2025 and increase funding for cluster munitions clearance.
Ethan Rinks told the Senior Minister that the Office of Arms Disarmament was in charge of clearing mines and explosive remnants of war, both regionally and globally. He added that for more than 30 years, the United States has helped Cambodia to clear about $ 190 million in landmines and explosive remnants of war, and will continue to assist Cambodia until Cambodia is mine-free by 2025.
Meanwhile, the Secretariat of Civil Aviation also released its report that in 2022, Cambodia will receive 29,942 flights from abroad. The destinations with the most flights to Cambodia are Vietnam, Thailand and Singapore.
The report added that 22,483 flights departed from Cambodia to the world, an increase of 16 percent, mostly to Vietnam, Thailand and Malaysia.
