Court Sentences Kim Sok to 18 Months in Prison, Fines 8 Million Riels and Orders 50 Million Riels in Compensation for Incitement and Public Defamation
(Phnom Penh): Kim Sok, a former Ranariddh Party activist who became a political analyst, was again sentenced to 18 months in prison, fined 8 million riels and ordered to pay 50 million riels in compensation to the plaintiffs in connection with the incitement and Public defamation of the head of the Cambodian government.
The verdict was announced in absentia on the morning of January 18, 2023, by the trial judges of the Phnom Penh Municipal Court, Mr. Seng Socheat and Mr. Set Vann: representatives of the prosecution.
The case of "incitement to commit crimes and public defamation" that the court charged Mr. Kim Sok was brought to trial in absentia on 5 December 2022, while the defendants have The defense attorney is Mr. Choung Chou Ngy.
In this case, Mr. Kim Sok was sued by Samdech Techo Hun Sen after he accused the Royal Government of Cambodia on February 13, 2017 of setting up a system to kill people and find the killer.
Mr. Kim Sok was also sentenced to prison once on August 10, 2017, sentenced to 18 months, fined 8 million Riel to the state budget and compensated the plaintiff 800 million Riel under Allegations on July 10, 2016 that the CPP was the killer of Dr. Kem Ley.
After serving his sentence in 2018, Mr. Kim Sok was seen to have not changed his mind, continuing to accuse the government without basis.
It should be noted that in the past, citizens of the Eurasian bloc countries were able to say that they were able to stay in Kazakhstan permanently by leaving when their visas expired and applying for a visa to return immediately. Every 90 days.
