Director of the Department of Private Security Management urges private security agencies to cooperate, provide information related to crime to the competent authorities and participate in the implementation of the policy "Safe villages, communes, sangkats" (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Private security agencies are considered a force by the National Police. Important assistance to the competent forces to participate in safeguarding security, order and order in society.
Seeing this, Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey, Director of the Private Security Management Department, urged all private security agencies stationed at various targets to cooperate in providing information related to crime to local law enforcement forces or specialized forces to take action. Prevent, prevent and suppress in a timely manner.
On the occasion of the opening of the 45th Private Security Agency Training Course on the morning of January 18, 2023, Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey said, "Leaders always pay attention to cracking down on online gambling crimes and "Trafficking in human beings, all forms of labor trafficking, therefore, the force of private security agents who are suspected of committing these crimes to report to local authorities, especially professional authorities."
In addition, Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey called on all private security agencies to participate in the implementation of the "Safe Village-Commune / Sangkat" policy, which is very important to make our village-commune safe. In particular, there is no crime of theft, robbery, drugs and illegal gambling to make us live in harmony.
According to Lt. Gen. Lim Sokha Raksmey, the private security sector is highly supported and cared for by Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, and Gen. Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner General, who has a strong vision for the purpose. Encourage the private security sector to grow to participate in maintaining security, safety, public order and society.
So far, the Department of Private Security Management of the General Commission of the National Police has conducted 45 private security agency refresher courses, equivalent to more than 7,000 private security agent trainees.
