Two endangered king fish, along with about 5,000 carp and trout, are no longer allowed to enter the Mekong River near the Royal Palace.
(Phnom Penh): Two endangered king fish, along with thousands of other endangered and ecologically important fish, are no longer in the wild at the confluence of the Tonle Sap and Mekong rivers. Cambodia is recognized as the last potential home of giant freshwater fish species of the Mekong River.
This indicates the alarming situation of this endangered species, whose numbers have declined by 99 percent in recent decades. Scientists hope this will lead to more joint conservation and coordination activities, as well as greater outreach to communities living along the Tonle Sap and Mekong rivers.
Both Mr. Neth Pheaktra and Mr. Has Sareth called on the people to participate in protecting and conserving natural resources, including rare fish released today, to allow them to reproduce and increase the number of rare fish in the Mekong River and Tonle Sap Lake. .
"It is imperative that we work together to preserve and conserve ecologically important and endangered species, such as the kingfisher, to maintain the well-being of the Mekong River," said Nguyen. This work will conserve natural ecosystems to support wildlife communities and economic activity throughout Cambodia. ”
"In addition to the conservation of these fish, the simultaneous efforts to protect the natural fish and their habitats are significant contributions to the reproduction of these fish in In nature.
The kingfisher has disappeared from all rivers except Cambodia. Today, countries in Southeast Asia are widely recognized as having the strongest hopes for the continued presence and resurgence of species and other Mekong giants such as carp, trout, trout. And cucumber fish.
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, through the Cambodian Fisheries Administration and the Inland Fisheries Research and Development Institute (IFReDI), is collaborating with the Mekong River Wonder Project to label and release many endangered species of fish.
Ms. Muy Chheng, representing the strong commitment of the local community, said, “I am happy that my family can contribute to the conservation of endangered fish. This is my husband's wish before he passed away. He wants to release these fish back into the Mekong River. ”
* Royal fish is the largest freshwater fish in the world. It can grow up to a maximum of 3 meters in length and weigh up to 300 kilograms, and is found only in the Mekong River. In Cambodia, however, a small number of king fish are still caught by fishing along the Tonle Sap, as well as from Cambodia's Mekong River.
* Cambodia's large, unobstructed rivers, along with the entire floodplain as a "pulse" that sustains life, contribute significantly to the conservation of the river's rich biodiversity and fisheries. Conservation of the kingfisher, an important fish species in the Mekong region, will help protect the livelihoods of millions of people who depend on productive fisheries in the river basin.
* USAID-funded Mekong Wonder Project works with governments, NGOs and partner communities to increase awareness of the importance of the Mekong River and to encourage the sustainable use of Cambodia's fisheries resources. National Committee for Disaster Management provides surface rescue equipment to 711 Brigade 711 Disaster Search and Rescue Operations
