Malaysia may suspend palm oil exports to EU after new ban
KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia said on Thursday it could suspend palm oil exports to the European Union in response to a new European law aimed at protecting forests by tightening controls on the sale of related products. And deforestation. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports that Minister of Commerce Fadillah Yusof said Malaysia and Indonesia would discuss laws banning the sale of palm oil and other deforestation-related goods, which the company says could be imported. To Europe only if those companies can show that their specific production does not destroy the forest.
As the European Union is a major importer of palm oil, the deal agreed this December drew criticism from Indonesia and Malaysia, two of the world's largest producers of palm oil.
"If we need foreign experts to oppose what our EU action is," Reuters quoted Fadillah as saying to reporters outside a seminar on Thursday. Do it. ”
"Otherwise, the choice is that we will just stop exporting to Europe, focusing only on other countries if they (the EU) give us a hard time exporting," he said. Hold their country. ”
Environmental activists have blamed the palm oil industry for causing massive deforestation in Southeast Asia, even though Indonesia and Malaysia have established mandatory sustainability certification standards for all plantations. The industry provides a lot of jobs and sources of income for small farmers.
