Senior FATF official encourages Cambodia to set an example in green finance
(Phnom Penh): In a courtesy call with Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen, senior officials of the FATF Finance Team encouraged Cambodia to continue to be a role model for other countries in the field of green finance.
According to Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo's personal assistant, during the meeting, she also congratulated Samdech Techo for taking measures to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 and successfully reopen the country and reopen the economy. .
Ms. Milin also highly valued Cambodia's commitment and encouraged Cambodia to lead other countries in the areas of green finance and green development.
In response, Samdech Techo welcomed Ms. Mi-Lin and the entire delegation to visit Cambodia. Samdech Techo also briefed the delegation on the strategy to prevent the spread of Kovid 19 and informed the delegation about the Green Development Initiative that Cambodia launched at the recent ASEAN Summit.
In October 2001, the FATF expanded its jurisdiction to combat the financing of terrorists in addition to the anti-money laundering campaign. In April 2012, the FATF stepped up its efforts against financing that contributed to the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction.
Since its inception, the FATF has operated under a specific mandate, requiring a specific decision by the Minister to continue its work. Three decades after its inception, in April 2019, the FATF Council of Ministers approved a new open term or indefinite term for the FATF.
The purpose of the FATF is to standardize and enhance the effective implementation of legal, regulatory and supportive measures to combat money laundering, terrorist financing, as well as to prevent any threats to the integrity of the international financial system.
The FATF monitors countries' progress in implementing the FATF recommendations, reviews anti-money laundering, terrorist financing and prevention measures, as well as promotes the adoption and implementation of the FATF globally.
The FATF currently has 37 full members and two regional organizations representing major financial centers from around the world.
