Ms. Phoeung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts, requested General Neth Savoeun, General Commissioner of the National Police, to take action to prevent the publication of the song entitled "Blood of Protesters".
Ms. Phoeung Sakona emphasized that the song "Blood of the Protesters" has provocative content and may cause insecurity and order to society.
After receiving this request, the Phnom Penh Municipal Police summoned four representatives of civil society to inquire about the posting of the song "Blood of Protesters".
The four representatives of civil society organizations and associations are: Mr. Moeun Tola, President of the Center for Labor Alliances and Human Rights, Mr. Am Sam Ath, Executive Director of LICADHO, Mr. Theng Savoeun, President of the Cambodian Farmers Community Coalition, Mr. Vorn Pov, President of the Solidarity House Union Original.
After questioning and questioning, LICADHO, Central and Union Solidarity House agreed to delete the music video from Facebook.
According to Ms. Phoeung Sakona, the song "Blood of the Protesters" was written and sung by Kea Sokun. The song has already been banned and convicted by the Ministry of Interior.
(Phnom Penh): About 44 years after Cambodia was liberated from the genocidal regime of Pol Pot on January 7, 1979, Cambodia has achieved remarkable national development. But for the anti-government group, it is still rumored that Cambodia has not made any progress.
During the groundbreaking ceremony for the National Road No. 7 from Skun to Kampong Cham on January 9, 2023, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, presented some important achievements in the field of road and bridge infrastructure. To be a testament to the development and progress in this field and for the people to have a basis to consider not to be deceived by the manipulation of the opposition.
The senior minister pointed out that after 1979, Cambodia had only one bridge across the river, the Chroy Changvar bridge, and it was further damaged. But now Cambodia has nearly 20 bridges across the river, and many roads are built interconnected from Phnom Penh and the provinces, a total of thousands of kilometers.
In addition, some roads such as National Road 6, National Road 3 and National Road 5 have also been expanded from two lanes to four lanes. At the same time, after the liberation in 1979, Cambodia did not have an expressway, but now Cambodia has an expressway from Phnom Penh to Sihanoukville.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol said that after the liberation in 1979, Cambodia had a small port for transporting goods that could no longer be used. But now Cambodia has international ports in Sihanoukville and Kien Svay in Kandal province.
Mr. Sun Chanthol also pointed out that after 1979, Cambodia had only one airport in Phnom Penh, which served to land almost no aircraft, but now Phnom Penh Airport has been upgraded and Cambodia has an airport in Siem Reap in Sihanoukville and Also in Koh Kong province.
For the senior minister, this is only a partial achievement in the field of public works and transportation. There are many other achievements that he has not mentioned yet.
