Cambodia-China Friendship Radio Senior Minister Sun Chanthol: No road or bridge construction project has been postponed or stopped due to the Kovid 19 crisis and the economic crisis (Video inside) (Kampong Cham): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, confirmed No road or bridge project was postponed or canceled due to the Kovid 19 crisis and the economic crisis.
The Kovid 19 crisis, as well as the global economic crisis caused by the war in Ukraine, has had a devastating effect on the economies of many countries in the world, including Cambodia. However, those crises did not hinder road and bridge construction projects in Cambodia. Countries that lend to infrastructure projects in Cambodia, such as China, Japan and South Korea, have not canceled any of their lending projects.
The senior minister also said that during the crisis, not only did it not affect the construction of roads and bridges, but the Royal Government of Cambodia turned this crisis into an opportunity for the construction of some infrastructure.
In line with the development of roads during the Kovid 19 crisis, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol stated, "If our government does not have the budget to save, it will not build 38 roads in Siem Reap." Now the road has been widened, there are bike lanes, there are pedestrian roads, the same as Sihanoukville, which shows the will of the Royal Government, the strength of its savings.
Please note that the construction of road and bridge infrastructure is still a priority policy for the Royal Government of Cambodia led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Before the end of 2022 and the beginning of 2023, four road and bridge projects using Chinese loans were inaugurated under the presidency of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Wang Vintian, Chinese Ambassador to Cambodia.
The four projects are: Rehabilitation and upgrading of National Road 41, National Road 31 and National Road 33, construction of a bridge across the Mekong River in Kratie province, and expansion and upgrading of National Road 7 from Skun to Kampong Cham city.
The construction of the four roads and bridges will cost a total of more than $ 400 million and will be completed simultaneously by 2026. China's new measures to prevent and control infectious diseases will make a significant contribution to the rehabilitation of tourism and infrastructure. World trade revives
BEIJING: After a three-year hiatus, Chinese expatriates and expatriates in China have resumed planning to travel abroad. From China, revised measures to prevent and control infectious diseases, stipulating that from January 8, 2023 onwards, the abolition of the Kovid test and the provocation of passengers in China.
This means that whether a Chinese citizen traveling abroad and returning or a foreigner wishing to enter Chinese territory, as long as a negative Kovid-19 certificate is tested 48 hours prior to departure Is able to participate in social activities after entering Chinese territory.
Remarkably, immediately after the introduction of the new measures, it was observed that the provinces and cities, China's main economic zones, arranged for foreign traders to travel directly to their investment destinations. Or to raise foreign investment, which is a sign that "discussions on online trade and investment between China and foreign countries" have gradually ended, instead of face-to-face talks.
China is the world's largest trading partner, becoming the largest trading partner of more than 120 countries and regions. Therefore, the development of the world economy is indispensable for the contribution of the Chinese economy. According to World Bank statistics, between 2013 and 2021, China's contribution to global economic growth averaged 38.6% per year, exceeding the G7. To more than 10%.
Especially in the context of the international situation has been facing repeated crises, China's economy has played an important role in maintaining the stability of the industrial chain and the global supply chain. In addition, after easing communicable disease prevention and control measures, it is believed that in 2023, China's economic growth will rebound, which may reach the level before the outbreak of the Kovid-19 crisis.
Experts and economists from many countries have assessed that China's new measures to prevent and control infectious diseases are a signal to the international community that travel between travelers and cross-border trade with China will increase. The return to ease and freedom as before the Kovid crisis is gradual, with the growth of China's economy and its trading partners in 2023 optimistic.
As we all know, Chinese tourists are an important source of tourism revenue for many countries in the world, especially in Southeast Asia. According to statistics, before the Kovid-19 crisis in 2019, about 155 million Chinese tourists went abroad, accounting for 10% of world tourism, bringing in about 258 billion US dollars. One-fifth of world tourism spending, a significant contribution to the GDP of the countries concerned.
Especially for Cambodia, a friend of steel, Chinese tourists who visited Cambodia in 2019, more than 2.3 million people, accounting for 35.7% of total international tourists, equivalent to about 1.8 billion US dollars in tourism revenue. A total of about $ 5 billion of Cambodia, while in 2019, Cambodia also received Chinese investors in tourism-related sectors, more than 50% of Cambodia's total foreign investment (FDI). This is according to data from the Ministry of Tourism of Cambodia.
The Chinese government has repeatedly stated that China's 19 measures to prevent and control the disease are based on a thorough scientific study of the actual situation. Most appropriate.
We can optimisticly expect that after China introduces new measures on the prevention and control of communicable diseases, namely the reopening of the country, it will contribute significantly to the revitalization of the world tourism and international trade. Back in 2023.
Article by: Tang Sileng, Staff of Cambodia-China Friendship Radio Cambodia continues to send 15 more explosive ordnance disposal forces to carry out humanitarian mission under UN umbrella in Central Africa
(Phnom Penh): On January 10, 2023, Cambodia continued to send 15 explosive ordnance disposal forces (98), a leading force to carry out humanitarian missions under the United Nations umbrella in the Central African Republic.
The farewell ceremony for the Cambodian Blue Hat Forces was held in the presence of Major General Prak Soth, Deputy Director General, Representative of General Sem Sovanny, Deputy Chairman of the National Commission and Director General of the National Peacekeeping Center, and other Blue Hat Army leaders at the airport. VIP aircraft.
Major General Prak Soth said that the deployment of 15 leading explosive ordnance disposal teams (98) now confirms the confidence gained from contributing to the peace process in conflict-affected countries like the previous divisions. Also trusted by the head of the Cambodian government, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, and Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, General Sem Sovanny.
In addition, Lt. Gen. Prak Soth advised all forces to consider safety first, especially during the performance of duties, not to underestimate any situation, and to learn from experience and good lessons, correct any mistakes. Happens to achieve success for the motherland, family and ourselves.
It should be noted that the 15 detonators of the 98th Explosive Ordnance Disposal Unit are responsible for liaising with UN, civilian and military officers in all specialized units and are responsible for setting up camps and receiving military equipment. Cars and machinery of the Cambodian Explosive Ordnance Disposal Unit, which is scheduled to arrive in the capital, Bangui in early February 2023.
In this context, Cambodia, which has gone through nearly three decades of war and only achieved full peace throughout the country in 1998 under the win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has integrated from within Asia, taking steps to expand the scope of experience. Its portfolio contributes to peacekeeping missions in nine countries, including 10 missions in Africa, the Middle East and Europe, from 2006 to the present and beyond.
