The following is a statement from the Regulatory Authority: China's central bank will increase support for private companies and ease crackdowns on technology companies.
BEIJING: China's central bank will increase support for private companies as part of economic stimulus measures while easing crackdowns on tech companies, said Guo Shuqing, chairman of the Communist Party of China (CPB). According to the Reuters news agency.
Guo told CCTV on Sunday that monetary policy in 2023 will focus on increasing demand, especially for personal consumption, Reuters reported.
Chinese leaders have vowed to build support for the world's second-largest economy, which was hit hard by the closure of Kovid-19 last year as well as declining global demand.
Guo, who is also chairman of the China Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission, said in an interview with CCTV that monetary policy should be coordinated with fiscal and social policies to increase incomes for the income group. Low and medium, as well as groups affected by Kovid.
"A prudent monetary policy will be clear and strong," Guo said. "That requires a focus on increasing demand effectively and deepening the supply chain structure."
He said monetary policy would strengthen support for private enterprises, maintain credit growth and reduce financing costs for businesses, and that Chinese financial institutions should treat all types of enterprises fairly.
Authorities aim to expand the financing network for private companies to support the issuance of their shares and bonds, Guo said.
Guo said China will also promote the proper development of Flash Form Online. He added that the adjustment of the 14 financial companies of Platform has been "basically completed" while some remaining issues need to be resolved.
Since the end of 2020, Beijing has increased its control over major Chinese financial technology companies.
