Cambodia-Australia launches market research document on Cambodian agricultural products for export to EU and Asean markets
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce and Mr. Pablo Kang, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, on January 9, 2023, jointly announced the launch of two books: 1) Cambodian agricultural products for export. To the EU and ASEAN markets; and 2) Cambodia and the opening up of the world market through free trade agreements. The ceremony was held at the Ministry of Commerce.
Both books were co-authored with the support of the Australian-Cambodian Agricultural Price Chain (CAVAC) program, which ended with a single digital nursery to be supported under another Australian new economic development program. Cambodia-Australia Partnership for Sustainable Economic Development (CAP RED).
Cambodian Agricultural Products for Export to EU and Asean Markets provides important insights into market demand, consumer demand, sanitary and phytosanitary measures and other non-tariff barriers to trade, product use, competitors Important and potential opportunities.
Crops and agricultural products included in this research book include: fresh mangoes, cashews, peppers, sweet potatoes, Pailin, palm sugar, bananas, avocados, sesame and processed fruits. This information, if used strategically, has the potential to create long-term benefits for Cambodian agriculture.
This information booklet will help agri-food enterprises, producers, exporters, business associations and other stakeholders to seize the opportunities of market diversification and reap the benefits under these free trade agreements.
The single digital nursery is planned to be developed according to Australia's own Free Trade Agreement website and will also be a comprehensive repository of trade information for exporters and importers to explore and take advantage of free trade agreements. All of Cambodia.
The digital nursery will also provide information on the rules of origin, the procedures for registering as an accredited exporter, and the procedure for obtaining a certificate of origin.
"Australia is pleased to assist Cambodia in this area to diversify its crops and export markets," he said. "We hope that these guides and the one digital nursery that will be developed in the future will make it easier for businesses / traders to access useful information to increase their business."
Mr. Pan Sosak emphasized that the Ministry of Commerce appreciated the support from the Australian Government and the CAP RED program for the development of a single digital nursery project.
"This project will help Cambodia to fully fulfill its commitments under a number of free trade agreements such as ATIGA, the Cambodia-Korea Free Trade Agreement, the ASEAN Free Trade Agreement, Australia, New Zealand and "RCEP agreement."
The two sides are committed to strengthening cooperation and promoting trade between Cambodia and Australia, and will continue to cooperate in other areas of mutual benefit, including the Cambodia-Australia Consumer Protection Partnership and Supported through the Regional Trade Program for Development (RT4D).
CAP RED is Australia's main economic development program in Cambodia worth A $ 87 million. CAP RED will support Cambodia in the use of the Free Trade Agreement, preparing for exit from the Least Developed Countries status, promoting trade and investment policies and practices, and building a system to increase trade. Karma and investment.
